  "worked_solution": "First, let's add a $Y={X}^{2}+1$ row to our table:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $5$ | $10$ | $17$ |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distribution of $Y$ is as follows:\n\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $5$ | $10$ | $17$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ |\n\nTherefore, $a=2,b=0.1,c=0.2,$ and $a+b+c=2.3.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
